e first. For each, decide what one row records and which business process or subject it serves.\n2. Start from saved areas. When a table fits an active saved area's definition, add it there instead of creating a new area; keep saved names and boundaries that still fit.\n3. Group the remaining tables by business capability: a process with its transactions, documents and events, plus the subjects it needs (Order Capture: order header, order line, order status, customer). Do not group by source System, schema, zone or name prefix alone.\n4. Keep together tables that one Logical Build must join: header and detail, a subject and its history or attributes, an association and both ends. Use table_links and Context records to confirm.\n5. Share reference and master tables: put a customer, product, calendar or currency table in every area whose process uses it. A table may belong to several areas; sharing is how areas connect.\n6. Size every area (see Area size), then split or merge along process boundaries until each area fits.\n7. Put a table in unassigned only when no evidence places it: technical, staging, log, audit or control tables, or tables whose meaning stays unclear. Do not invent an area to empty unassigned.\n8. Change a saved area only to correct or extend it, and retire one only when its capability no longer applies.\n\n## Area size\n\nAn area is the unit of one Logical Build call, so size decides quality: an area that is too big overloads one call, and one that is too small splits a process that must be designed together.\n\n- Aim for about 5 to 12 tables and a few hundred columns per area (sum column_count). Up to about 20 tables is fine when they are small and tightly joined; a lone very wide table can be an area by itself.\n- Split an area above roughly 400 columns or 20 tables along process boundaries (Order Capture, Fulfilment, Invoicing), keeping each process's header, detail and subjects together. Never split a header from its lines or a subject from its history to make sizes even.\n- Merge tiny areas: a one- or two-table area belongs in the capability that uses it, unless it is a distinct business capability with its own process.\n- Shared reference tables count in every area that lists them; share a reference table rather than adding it to an area whose process does not use it.\n- Let the business decide the number of areas: a 40-table scope usually has about 3 to 8. If this run has fewer tables than one good area, adding them to fitting saved areas, or one new area, is right.\n\n## Answer shape\n\n- areas: new areas and saved areas you change. name: a short business name in Title Case, unique case-insensitively. definition: one or two sentences on the capability the area covers and its boundary; required for a new area, omit it to keep a saved one.\n- tables: every table from table_index that belongs in the area. For a saved area the list replaces its saved tables from this run; its other_table_count members and its locked tables stay whatever you list. Saved areas you leave out stay exactly as saved, so do not repeat unchanged areas.\n- unassigned: every table from table_index that is in no active area after your answer. Each table is in at least one area or in unassigned, never both.\n- retire: saved area names to mark inactive with their tables; nothing is deleted. Do not list an area and retire it. Locked areas, areas with locked tables and areas with other_table_count above 0 cannot retire in this run: leave them out.\n- Return empty areas and retire, with every table not in a saved active area in unassigned, only when no grouping is supported.\n\n## Repair\n\nOn repair, fix every context.repair.validation_issues entry and keep valid work from repair.previous_candidate; return the complete corrected answer, not a patch. If previous_candidate_omitted is true, rebuild from the evidence. Paths point into your answer (areas[2].tables[5], unassigned[0], retire[1]). table_unaccounted lists up to 50 missing tables in source_refs with their table ids: place each in an area or in unassigned. retire_outside_run names an area that still holds tables outside this run: keep it instead.\n\n## Frozen evidence readers\n\ntable_index, source_context and gds_context are inline; get_source_context and get_gds_context repeat them, so do not call them. Before answering, read get_scope_tables, get_table_links, get_subject_areas and get_context_records with no selector through the last page.\n\nReaders return items with next_cursor/is_complete. Continue a chain with the returned cursor alone; a null selector (omit it instead), a selector added to a cursor, another reader's cursor, an unknown key or malformed arguments fail the whole run. Each call uses one of limited turns (default 50 per attempt) and each page stays in context; exhausting either fails the run. A reply without a reader call is your final answer, so finish every read first. A repair attempt starts without earlier reader results; re-read what fixes need. A complete empty result means none exist.",
    "instruction_prompt": "<table_index>\n{{ table_index }}\n</table_index>\n\n<source_context>\n{{ source_context }}\n</source_context>\n\n<gds_context>\n{{ gds_context }}\n</gds_context>\n\n## Task\n\nGroup this run's tables into Subject Areas from inline and reader evidence with the ordered design and area size guidance in the system message.\nBefore returning, confirm: every table id exists in table_index; every table is in an active area after your answer (listed, or saved and left out) or in unassigned, never both; every area is a coherent capability of a workable size (about 5 to 12 tables, a few hundred columns) unless the evidence says otherwise; every new area has a definition; no locked area or locked table changes; each area name appears once.",
