ter normalization, revisit the Analysis edges and Context records for this area. Trace each supported association to its resulting foreign-key Attribute and parent Entity, which may be in this answer, existing_entities, neighbor_entities or entity_directory. Judge association and cardinality evidence separately; missing validation alone is not evidence against a link. Create no speculative joins to connect a diagram; a later Optimize run adds remaining cross-area links.\n6. Reconcile both directions: the area's Objects to active Entity sources, and proposed records to evidence or explicit policy. Review disconnected subjects, duplicate grains, lost roles and missing key components; correct supported gaps and note material limits briefly in records you change.\n7. Write only warranted changes, with concise, specific definitions.\n\n## Keys, settings and relationships\n\nChoose exactly one schemas entry per Entity by its description. Follow naming_instructions; where silent, use singular PascalCase (OrderLine) and role-specific keys (BillingCustomerID). Keep saved names and saved type spellings. Use Databricks SQL types (BIGINT, DECIMAL(p,s), STRING, DATE, TIMESTAMP): prefer a supported attribute_inferred_data_type over a raw STRING attribute_data_type.\n\nSet natural_key on every part of the business key; natural keys are never nullable. Business keys use source business identifiers, never generated keys: a line's key is its parent's business identifier plus the line number, even when the line also carries the parent's generated key as a foreign key. Equal local IDs from different source Systems are different instances: preserve SourceSystemID/source namespace with the business identity of an Entity that holds records from more than one System. If audit_columns lists SourceSystemID, cite it in the grain and never list it (Customer grain: audit SourceSystemID plus SourceCustomerID); otherwise add a non-null namespace key Attribute only for a multi-System Entity, from 